00:46:01.400 --> 00:46:13.580
Now just for reference, a 20-day
exponential moving average can also be
referred to as the 9.52% EMA.

00:46:13.580 --> 00:46:28.020
So that's our multiplier, the 0.0952. And
the 10-day exponential moving average can
be referred to as the 18.18% EMA.

00:46:28.020 --> 00:46:36.300
Multiplier for the 10-day exponential
moving average is 0.1818.

00:46:36.300 --> 00:46:50.640
So for our time period as long-short
portfolio managers over 20, 60 days, we
are using 20, 60, 120, and 250-day EMAs.

00:46:50.640 --> 00:47:11.920
So for 20 days, we have what's termed as a
9.52% EMA, for 60 days, a 3.27% EMA, for
120 days, a 1.65% EMA, and 250 days, a
0.79% EMA.

00:47:11.920 --> 00:47:21.260
Now, before we look at examples on a chart
of exponential moving averages, we're
actually going to look at our fourth
momentum indicator,

00:47:21.820 --> 00:47:24.580
which is moving average convergence
divergence.

00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:34.720
And this is because the MACD, the moving
average convergence divergence
calculation, draws on the exponential
moving average data.

00:47:35.200 --> 00:47:45.080
So the MACD indicator is what's termed as
a momentum oscillator that measures the
difference in two EMA periods.

00:47:45.480 --> 00:47:54.200
So the MACD is calculated by subtracting
the longer-term EMA from the shorter-term
EMA.

00:47:54.480 --> 00:47:58.420
And this creates what's called the MACD
line.

00:47:59.280 --> 00:48:10.040
And the MACD line fluctuates above and
below a 0 line as the EMAs converge,
cross, and diverge from each other.

00:48:11.040 --> 00:48:22.520
On top of having the MACD line and the 0
line, there's also a third line called the
signal line that's created by taking an
EMA of the MACD itself.

00:48:23.480 --> 00:48:40.600
So the formula that you're always going to
bump into, the default formula for MACD
that will typically be the default on
websites and in any software that you
download for technical analysis and price
action,

00:48:41.440 --> 00:48:49.140
the default formula for MACD is the
12-period EMA minus the 26-period EMA.

00:48:49.480 --> 00:48:59.420
So this gives the MACD line, and then a
nine-day EMA of the MACD line itself is
worked out as the signal line.

00:49:00.400 --> 00:49:14.600
So using a 12- and 26-day EMA is the most
commonly distributed method of
calculation, and pretty much all retail
traders use this method and follow it
almost like a religion.

00:49:15.760 --> 00:49:29.620
But again, as with so many things in
technical analysis and price action
indicators, no one ever seems to ask what
the significance is of 12, 26, and nine
days.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:43.500
The only remote or close to significance
would be in the Forex markets, where one
week is six days, two weeks is 12 days,
and nine days is randomly one and a half
weeks.

00:49:44.020 --> 00:49:57.860
But then again, that kind of leaves us
hanging dry when we try to explain what
these numbers mean in terms of their
significance, because it just leaves us
asking, why 26 days?

00:49:58.240 --> 00:49:59.540
What's the significance there?

00:50:00.540 --> 00:50:03.800
So even in the Forex markets, there's a
bit of randomness there.

